WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:09.169
For the next part of the exercise, we are going to add some locking functionality to our door blueprint.

2
00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:16.170
To do this within the BP door blueprint, we're going to want to control the colour of the point light.

3
00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:24.620
So we're going to want it to be red when the light is locked, and we're going to want it to be green when it is unlocked.

4
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.620
Now to do this, I'm going to create my own function.

5
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:33.619
And this function we're going to use, all it's going to do is update the light.

6
00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:39.000
So I'm going to come over into the function section here, and I'm going to push plus.

7
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
That's going to give me a new function section.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.030
And now I'm going to type in update light.

9
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:54.609
Now, in this update light, I'm going to check to see if the door is locked or unlocked.

10
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:57.609
And I'm going to switch the light accordingly.

11
00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:05.859
Now, in order for us to save the state of the door, we're going to need a variable.

12
00:01:05.859 --> 00:01:11.959
So I'm going to hit variable. I'm going to call this is locked. Question.

13
00:01:11.959 --> 00:01:18.689
And now this is a boolean variable, so it is either true or false.

14
00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:26.349
I'm going to drag this in, and I'm going to get is locked.

15
00:01:26.349 --> 00:01:31.349
And then I'm going to drag off of the boolean and type in branch.

16
00:01:31.349 --> 00:01:38.159
All it's going to do if this is locked, it's going to do one thing.

17
00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:42.159
And if it is not locked, it's going to do something else.

18
00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:45.609
Now we're going to want to update the point light.

19
00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:50.959
So I'm going to drag the point light in to get a reference.

20
00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:53.599
You now see we have a point light.

21
00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:59.790
And from that point light, I'm going to do set color.

22
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.790
And that's going to give us the ability to set the light color.

23
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.790
Now I'm going to plug this into true to start with.

24
00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:10.979
And under the new light color, I am going to select.

25
00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.020
So this is true for if it is locked.

26
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.300
So if it is locked, I'm going to make it all right.

27
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.659
I'm going to click OK.

28
00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:26.139
Now I'm going to copy this whole section, make a duplicate here.

29
00:02:26.139 --> 00:02:31.419
And I will change the color to green.

30
00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:35.090
Just drag that down.

31
00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.090
And we have a green light.

32
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.090
So this is a simple function.

33
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.090
What it will do is it will update the light.

34
00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:44.090
It's just going to check the variable is locked.

35
00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.090
And if it's true, it will set it to red.

36
00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.090
And if it's false, it's going to set it to green.

37
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:56.340
Now, I want to do something special with the is locked variable.

38
00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.340
If we look down in the bottom left-hand corner,

39
00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.340
there is a little eye that can be clicked on.

40
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.340
And that will open up the eye.

41
00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.340
Now, this will also be referenced to the tick box

42
00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.729
in the details panel for instance editable.

43
00:03:11.729 --> 00:03:15.729
And instance editable means that we can manually change this

44
00:03:15.729 --> 00:03:19.729
on each instance that we add into the world.

45
00:03:19.729 --> 00:03:24.370
Now, I'll demonstrate that in a second.

46
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.370
The first thing is we've created a function,

47
00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.370
but we haven't told the system to run it at all.

48
00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:34.719
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to come into the construction script here.

49
00:03:34.719 --> 00:03:38.909
And I am going to drag off from the construction script.

50
00:03:38.909 --> 00:03:42.909
And I'm going to type in update light.

51
00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:47.909
So that's going to call this on construction script.

52
00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:51.680
Now, there's one more thing that I want to do.

53
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.680
And that is I'm going to jump back to the event graph.

54
00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.680
And now I'm going to get rid of my print string.

55
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.780
And I'm going to add a similar branching again.

56
00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.889
So I will add a branch in.

57
00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:09.889
And I will drag the is locked variable

58
00:04:09.889 --> 00:04:12.979
and place it down there.

59
00:04:12.979 --> 00:04:15.979
Now, we only want the door to open and close

60
00:04:15.979 --> 00:04:19.980
if it is not locked. So is locked is false.

61
00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.980
We will go to the flip-flop.

62
00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.980
If it's true, it's going to do nothing.

63
00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.980
Okay, so we've done a few things there.

64
00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.980
We've created a new function which will update lights called update lights.

65
00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.980
And we have connected that to the construction script.

66
00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.069
And then on the event graph, we have added a branch in

67
00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:46.069
that will allow us to stop the functionality

68
00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:50.360
if the variable is set to false.

69
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.360
So let's compile this jump into the world.

70
00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:03.540
Now, if I was to come in and create a second version of the door,

71
00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.139
I'm going to drag this out holding down alt.

72
00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:14.649
And I will move that so it's next door to it.

73
00:05:14.649 --> 00:05:17.649
I'm going to delete the gun. We do not need it for this.

74
00:05:17.649 --> 00:05:23.089
Now, you'll see when I have one instance of this selected,

75
00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:26.089
I have an option for is locked in the details panel.

76
00:05:26.089 --> 00:05:30.089
Now, if I click that on, you can see that the light turns red.

77
00:05:30.089 --> 00:05:33.250
And that's because in the construction script,

78
00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:37.250
it's triggered the update light function and the light is changed.

79
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:44.009
And now if I run the game, if I walk up to this door and press E,

80
00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:49.009
it will do nothing. However, this door will open and close.

81
00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.100
It's very straightforward, but we are just able to update

82
00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:57.100
and control the functionality on different instances of the same blueprint.

83
00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:06.060
Great. So we have now added in a locking functionality to our doors.

84
00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.180
And we've done that by using the construction script

85
00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:13.540
and creating a function that we can reuse called the update light.

86
00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:16.540
Now, in the next video, what we're going to do

87
00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:21.540
is we're going to create another actor with a button

88
00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.540
which can unlock or lock the doors.
